<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jânio Quadros</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jânio Quadros was a Brazilian politician who rose rapidly from mayor to governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> campaigning against corruption, with a broom as his symbol. He won the presidential election of 1960 by a large margin, but after an erratic seven months in office, marked by an independent foreign policy, he resigned unexpectedly on 25 August 1961. The resignation provoked a crisis over the succession of his vice-president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joao-goulart">João Goulart</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/janio-quadros.jpg" alt="Jânio Quadros" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Governo do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%A2nio_Quadros,_presidente_do_Brasil_em_1961_(foto_oficial).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
